96:## Setting up a batch's worktrees
97-
98-The workflow script cannot touch the filesystem before its first agent runs, so
99-the coordinator creates the worktrees and pushes the empty branches first, from
100-the commit `main` is at, which is then the script's `args.base`:
101-
102-```sh
103-cd /home/user/fortress && BASE=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)
104-for r in r1-atomic-static r2-literal-wrap; do
105-  git worktree add -b wip/repair-$r /home/user/fortress-${r%%-*} main
106-  cp -a ProjectFortress/build /home/user/fortress-${r%%-*}/ProjectFortress/   # javac incremental; scalac rebuilds anyway
107-  mkdir -p /home/user/fortress-${r%%-*}/tmp
108-  git push -u origin wip/repair-$r
109-done
110-```
111-
112-Copy the build, never symlink it: a symlinked build makes `ProjectProperties`
113-resolve every cache path to the main tree (`batched-climb-plan.md` §4). After
114-the batch lands, the commit stage removes the worktrees and local branches; the
115-remote `wip/` branches are deleted in the GitHub UI.
116-
117-## The branch trap
118-
119-The infrastructure clones the branch the session was created from. If work
120-proceeds on `main` while that branch is left behind, a re-provision lands on a
121-stale tree — or fails outright if the branch was renamed or deleted, which is
122-what ended the 2026-09-17 session: *"The requested branch or commit was not
123-found in the repository."* Keep the container's own branch fast-forwarded to
124-`main` whenever `main` moves.
125-
126-## Reading another session's transcript
127-
128-The orphan branches are in the same repository, so any session can read any
129-other's without switching or re-cloning:
130-
131-```sh
132-git fetch origin transcripts
133-git show origin/transcripts:projects/-home-user-fortress/<session>.jsonl.parts/000.jsonl | head
134-git grep -l 'some string' origin/transcripts -- 'projects/*'
135-```
